-[0m[32mAbsolutely, yes. This is a crucial aspect of comprehensive anti-bullying policy. Young people are often targeted based on how others *perceive* their identity, regardless of how they actually identify. A student who appears gender non-conforming or who peers think might be LGBTQ+ faces real harassment and discrimination.
-[0m[32m
-[0m[32mOur schools have a responsibility to protect all students from bullying based on sexual orientation or gender expression—perceived or otherwise. This isn't about making assumptions about identity; it's about preventing harm based on appearance, presentation, or stereotypes others project onto students.
-[0m[32m
-[0m[32mWhen we only protect students after they've publicly come out or declared an identity, we're leaving vulnerable young people unprotected during critical moments. Many youth are still exploring their identity or haven't felt safe enough to come out. They deserve the same protections.
-[0m[32m
-[0m[32mInclusive anti-bullying policies also send an important message: our schools value and protect all students, including those who don't fit traditional gender norms. This contributes to a more equitable, inclusive environment where everyone can learn safely. We know from research that students in supportive school environments have better mental health outcomes and academic performance. Protecting students from discrimination based on perceived identity is both a matter of social justice and practical educational policy.<|im_end|>[0m[33m
