-[0m[32mThat's a common argument, but I think it misunderstands both the First Amendment and the nature of public accommodation laws. The First Amendment protects your right to hold beliefs and practice your religion—it doesn't protect you from having to follow neutral, generally applicable laws in the public sphere.
