-[0m[32mThat's an excellent question. I believe we need several key reforms. First, we should streamline adoption processes and reduce costs—many adoptions run $20,000-$40,000, which puts them out of reach for middle-class families. Tax credits and subsidies could help. Second, we need to strengthen the foster care system by improving caseworker training and reducing caseloads so children get proper oversight. Third, we should incentivize adoption through employer benefits and make maternity care more affordable so women aren't forced into abortion by financial pressure. Fourth, faith-based and private organizations should be empowered to participate fully in adoption services—they've historically done excellent work. Finally, we need cultural emphasis on adoption as a beautiful alternative. When women facing difficult pregnancies see adoption as genuinely supported by society—not just as an abstract option—more will choose life. The fundamental principle here is personal responsibility paired with community and family support, not government dependency.<|im_end|>[0m[33m
