-[0m[32mThat's a practical question, and the honest answer is: allied militaries handle it very smoothly with minimal cost or complication.
-[0m[32m
-[0m[32mCanada and the UK, for example, simply treat bathroom and housing accommodations the same way they treat any personnel with medical needs or individual preferences—case by case, pragmatically. Most modern military facilities have private or semi-private facilities anyway, so the actual infrastructure changes are minimal. In many cases, it requires no renovation at all.
-[0m[32m
-[0m[32mThe cost argument doesn't hold up empirically. Canada's transition has been negligible in budget terms. Healthcare costs for transition-related care are modest compared to overall military medical spending—we're talking about a small percentage of what we already spend on other medical treatments.
-[0m[32m
-[0m[32mWhat's important to recognize is that this concern, while framed as practical, often reflects anxiety about change rather than genuine logistical barriers. We manage accommodations for religious observance, dietary restrictions, and various medical conditions all the time.
-[0m[32m
-[0m[32mBeyond logistics, we should ask: what kind of society do we want to be? One that tells qualified, patriotic Americans "you can't serve your country openly because accommodating you is inconvenient"? That fundamentally conflicts with our values of equality and dignity.
-[0m[32m
-[0m[32mWhen we prioritize inclusion and treat all service members with respect, we actually strengthen recruitment and retention. We access talent we were previously excluding. The evidence from allied militaries shows this works. It's not complicated—it just requires a commitment to treating all our people fairly.<|im_end|>[0m
