-[0m[32mThat's an excellent question that gets at the heart of protecting both mother and child. From a conservative perspective, we should focus on market-driven and voluntary solutions that empower women with genuine choices.
-[0m[32m
-[0m[32mFirst, we need to reduce unnecessary government regulations that drive up adoption costs—often $20,000 to $40,000. By streamlining licensing requirements and allowing private agencies more flexibility, we can lower barriers and increase accessibility. Second, we should expand tax credits and adoption subsidies to make it more affordable for families, creating demand that serves both sides.
-[0m[32m
-[0m[32mThird, cultural reform matters enormously. We should actively promote adoption through community organizations, churches, and civil society rather than relying on government campaigns. This restores the dignity adoption deserves without stigma.
-[0m[32m
-[0m[32mFourth, transparent counseling is crucial—women need unbiased information about all options including adoption, without pressure from any direction. Finally, we should strengthen birthmother privacy protections and post-adoption support services so women feel secure in their choice.
-[0m[32m
-[0m[32mThe key principle is personal responsibility and voluntary choice within strong communities—not government coercion in any direction. When we make adoption genuinely accessible and respectable, it becomes a real choice that honors both the sanctity of life and maternal dignity.<|im_end|>[0m[33m
