-[0m[32mAbsolutely, this is a crucial step toward ensuring equal access to family formation. Transparent reporting would shed light on how religious exemptions are actually being used and who gets excluded. Right now, many agencies operate with minimal oversight, and we have no comprehensive data on how often these exemptions deny service to LGBTQ+ couples, interfaith families, or other groups. When we require agencies receiving public funding or operating under state licenses to report their practices, we create accountability. This data would reveal systemic patterns—whether certain communities face disproportionate barriers, how often exemptions are invoked, and what the real-world impact is on vulnerable children waiting for homes. We should remember that adoption serves the child's best interest, and every qualified, loving family strengthens our communities. Transparency also protects religious freedom itself—it allows legitimate religious practice while preventing discrimination disguised as religious conviction. This is fundamentally about equity and ensuring public resources don't fund exclusion.<|im_end|>[0m[33m
