-[0m[32mThat's a thoughtful follow-up. The goal isn't to eliminate faith-based agencies—many do excellent work—but to establish clear standards that all publicly-funded entities should meet. We can structure transparency requirements to be reasonable and non-punitive. First, reporting requirements themselves don't force anyone out; they simply document practices. Agencies wouldn't face consequences just for having religious beliefs—they'd face accountability if they're using exemptions to discriminate. Second, we should distinguish between agencies that serve all families regardless of faith and those with explicitly religious missions. An agency can absolutely prioritize faith-aligned placements or maintain religious values without denying service to others. The real issue is when public resources subsidize blanket exclusions. Third, we could offer technical support and capacity-building grants to help agencies adapt their practices while maintaining their values. We do this in healthcare, education, and other sectors. Finally, transparency often leads to dialogue—communities can work together to find solutions rather than operating in silos. The integrity of the adoption system depends on it serving all children equally. We can uphold both religious conscience and equal protection; they're not mutually exclusive when we prioritize children's welfare.<|im_end|>[0m
