-[0m[33m<|im_start|>assistant
-[0m[32mThat's an important follow-up. If we're serious about being pro-life, we must back it up with real support. Here are concrete policies I'd advocate for:
-[0m[32m
-[0m[32mFirst, strengthen the adoption system and make it more affordable. Tax credits for adoption costs, streamlined processes, and better counseling can make adoption a genuine option without the financial burden.
-[0m[32m
-[0m[32mSecond, expand and improve pregnancy resource centers that provide free ultrasounds, prenatal care coordination, and material support. These should be well-funded and help women understand all their options.
-[0m[32m
-[0m[32mThird, promote employer-based benefits—extended parental leave, flexible work arrangements, and childcare assistance. When companies compete for talent, these become standard offerings. Rather than government mandates, market incentives work better.
-[0m[32m
-[0m[32mFourth, reform healthcare to make prenatal and birth costs more transparent and affordable. Price transparency and competition reduce costs far better than government bureaucracy.
-[0m[32m
-[0m[32mFifth, strengthen the tax code to reward childbearing—expanded child tax credits that don't penalize marriage or single mothers, and credits for dependent care.
-[0m[32m
-[0m[32mSixth, promote mentoring programs and community support networks through churches and civic organizations—this addresses isolation and emotional struggles.
-[0m[32m
-[0m[32mThe conservative approach is enabling women through practical freedom and community support, not government dependency. We trust families and voluntary institutions more than centralized welfare programs. When women have genuine options and support, most choose life.<|im_end|>[0m
