-[0m[32mHowever—and I think this is the critical part—the argument against broad exemptions is that they shift the burden of someone's religious beliefs onto vulnerable people. When a business owner gets an exemption, it's LGBTQ+ customers, women seeking reproductive care, or people from other faiths who face discrimination. These aren't abstract principles—they're real people being denied housing, healthcare, employment, or services based on identity they can't change.
