-[0m[32mThat's the central disagreement, and I understand the intuitive appeal of that position. But I think it misses something crucial about what happens when you operate a business in the public sphere.
-[0m[32m
-[0m[32mWhen you choose to operate a business that serves the general public—a bakery, a pharmacy, a hotel, a healthcare clinic—you're entering into a social contract. You're benefiting from public infrastructure, public education that trained your employees, public roads, police and fire protection. In exchange, society asks that you don't use that business as a tool to discriminate.
-[0m[32m
-[0m[32mThere's a real difference between what you believe privately and what you can do as a public business. I can believe whatever I want in my home, in my church, in my private life. But once I'm running a business that serves the public, other people's rights matter too.
