-[0m[32mLet's be concrete: if a pharmacist can refuse to fill birth control prescriptions on religious grounds, women lose access to essential healthcare. If a hospital can refuse emergency abortion care, pregnant people in medical crises die. If landlords can refuse to rent to LGBTQ+ couples, vulnerable people become homeless. These aren't hypothetical—they're happening.
-[0m[32m
-[0m[32mThe real conscience question should be: if your faith prevents you from providing a service, should you open a business providing that service to the public? There are paths forward—hire staff who don't share your objection, refer to another provider, operate a faith-based organization openly.
-[0m[32m
-[0m[32mWe protect conscience, but not the right to impose your conscience on others seeking basic services. That's the balance that ensures collective welfare and equal dignity for everyone.<|im_end|>[0m
