-[0m[32mHere's my thinking: First, on principle, many Americans have deep moral and religious convictions that abortion ends a human life. Forcing taxpayers to fund something that violates their conscience is fundamentally unjust. It's a matter of basic freedom and personal responsibility—those who support abortion access should fund it through private means, not government coercion.
-[0m[32m
-[0m[32mSecond, government has a limited constitutional role. Healthcare decisions are best made by individuals, families, and their doctors—not bureaucracies managing inflation-indexed entitlements. We've seen how government programs grow, become inefficient, and expand beyond their original scope.
-[0m[32m
-[0m[32mThird, there are alternatives. Private charities, community organizations, and voluntary contributions can address healthcare needs without the coercive power of taxation. This respects both conscience rights and individual liberty.
-[0m[32m
